# Licensing

This repository holds material with four different licensing
dispositions. Which one governs a given file is **derived from recorded
provenance**, not from a list maintained by hand: run

    python tools/licensing/disposition.py

to print the disposition of every tracked file, or

    python tools/licensing/disposition.py --list project-content

to list one category. `tests/licensing/test_disposition.py` requires every
tracked file to fall under exactly one.

---

## 1. Project-authored content — CC BY 4.0

The BFO-aligned ontology modules, their annotations, project
documentation, diagrams, and site content.

    SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-4.0

Full text: [LICENSES/CC-BY-4.0.txt](LICENSES/CC-BY-4.0.txt).
Summary: <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/>

You may share and adapt this material, including commercially, provided
you give appropriate credit.

## 2. Project-authored software — Apache License 2.0

Code, tests, build tools, generators, checkers, repository
configuration, and CI workflows.

    SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

Full text: [LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt](LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt).

Apache-2.0 is permissive and includes an express patent grant.

## 3. Third-party material — under its own licence

Vendored upstream dependencies, redistributed under the terms their
authors set. This repository grants nothing over them and changes
nothing about them.

| material | licence | full text |
|---|---|---|
| Basic Formal Ontology (BFO) | CC BY 4.0 | [LICENSES/CC-BY-4.0.txt](LICENSES/CC-BY-4.0.txt) |
| Common Core Ontologies (CCO) extract | BSD-3-Clause | [LICENSES/BSD-3-Clause.txt](LICENSES/BSD-3-Clause.txt) |

See [THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md](THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md).

## 4. Original ValueNet material — no licence identified, no rights granted

The original ValueNet corpus, trigger frames, folk-value fragments, and
associated data descend from upstream work whose licence this repository
has **not been able to identify**.

**No rights are granted over this material by this repository.** Nothing
here relicenses it, and its presence in this repository is not a
statement that it may be reused. It is excluded from the public download
bundle until its redistribution terms are established. If you want to use
it, establish the terms with the upstream authors.

This is the largest category in the repository by file count. That is a
fact about where ValueNet came from, not an oversight.

---

## Why the split

The two project licences are the most permissive terms this project can
lawfully grant: broad reuse including commercial use and modification,
with attribution. No NonCommercial, NoDerivatives, ShareAlike,
source-availability, or field-of-use restriction is imposed.

"Most permissive" is bounded by what is ours to grant. It does not
extend to third-party material and does not extend to upstream content
whose terms are unknown — relicensing either by omission would be the
opposite of permissive.

## How a file's disposition is decided

From **recorded provenance plus explicit owner adjudications**.

Provenance does most of the work: `config/move-manifest.yaml` records
each file's origin, frozen and reviewed before this licensing work
began. A tracked file the manifest does not mention is confirmed absent
from a recorded upstream snapshot before it is treated as fork-authored.

That snapshot is a specific commit, named in
`config/upstream-snapshot.yaml`, and never a branch. A remote-tracking
branch moves when somebody else pushes, which would let the licence of a
file here change with no commit in this repository and let two clones
disagree about the same file. The snapshot is an ancestor of this
repository's history, so any full clone can reproduce the classification
with no remote configured.

Provenance answers where a file came from. A licence question asks who
wrote what is there now, and occasionally those diverge. Where they do,
the answer is a judgement rather than a measurement, so it is made once,
by the owner, for a named file, and recorded in `ADJUDICATIONS` in
`tools/licensing/disposition.py` with the evidence it rests on. The
historical origin is preserved, never rewritten.

There is currently one adjudication:

| file | ruling | evidence |
|---|---|---|
| `README.md` | project content, CC BY 4.0 | rewritten in full at commit `39a8001`; shares one line with the upstream original, and that line is a bare markdown code fence |

This is deliberately **not** a similarity threshold. A rule of the form
"below N% shared lines counts as fork-authored" would apply itself to
files nobody adjudicated, and relicensing somebody else's work by
heuristic is the failure the whole classifier exists to avoid. Every
other file descended from upstream ValueNet — the folk fragments among
them — remains unresolved and excluded, and a test asserts exactly that.
